       ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
  <section id="history-of-timezones" class="content-section reveal" aria-label="History of Time Zones">
    <h2>2. The History of Time Zones</h2>
    <p>
      Prior to the mid-19th century, timekeeping was entirely local. Every town and city set their own clocks based on the sun's position, meaning solar noon was calibrated locally. While this was sufficient for agricultural communities, it created severe challenges during the industrial revolution as high-speed transport and telecommunications emerged:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>The Railway Time Revolution</strong>: The expansion of railroads in Great Britain during the 1840s created complex scheduling hazards. A train traveling from London to Bristol had to navigate differing local clocks, making timetables nearly impossible to coordinate and resulting in head-on collisions. To resolve this, Great Britain adopted "Railway Time" (Greenwich Mean Time) in 1847, standardizing all station clocks to GMT. In the United States, railroads established a four-zone standard on November 18, 1883 (the "Day of Two Noons"), replacing over 100 local railroad time systems. Before this, Boston clocks were 11 minutes ahead of New York clocks, and Washington D.C. was 7 minutes behind Philadelphia.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Sir Sandford Fleming (1876-1879)</strong>: In 1876, Canadian railway engineer Sir Sandford Fleming missed a train in Ireland due to a misprinted timetable showing p.m. instead of a.m. This experience inspired him to propose a worldwide system of 24 standard time zones, each centered on a meridian of longitude 15 degrees apart, using a single global 24-hour clock.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>International Meridian Conference (1884)</strong>: Representatives from 26 nations met in Washington D.C. to establish a single Prime Meridian. They selected the longitude passing through the Royal Observatory in Greenwich, London, as the 0° reference line. This conference established the foundation of modern time zones, dividing the globe into 24 theoretical hour-wide longitudinal belts.
      </li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
       SECTION 3: 15-DEGREE RULE
       ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
  <section id="15-degree-rule" class="content-section reveal" aria-label="The 15-Degree Rule">
    <h2>3. How Time Zones Work: The 15-Degree Rule</h2>
    <p>
      The Earth is a sphere that rotates 360 degrees on its axis approximately every 24 hours. Dividing the total degrees by the duration of the rotation yields the basic math of global time:
    </p>
    <p>
      {"$$\\frac{360^{\\circ}}{24\\text{ hours}} = 15^{\\circ}\\text{ per hour}$$"}
    </p>
    <p>
      This means that for every 15 degrees of longitude traveled east or west from the Prime Meridian, local solar time changes by exactly one hour. Traveling east moves clocks forward (+1 hour), while traveling west moves clocks backward (-1 hour).
    </p>

    <!-- Custom Inline SVG: World Time Zone Meridian Grid (Infographic 1) -->
