  <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
       SECTION 4: UTC VS GMT
       ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
  <section id="utc-vs-gmt" class="content-section reveal" aria-label="UTC vs GMT">
    <h2>4. UTC vs. GMT: The Core Differences</h2>
    <p>
      Although Greenwich Mean Time (GMT) and Coordinated Universal Time (UTC) share the same offset values, they represent completely distinct systems:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>Coordinated Universal Time (UTC)</strong>: A scientific time standard regulated by hundreds of high-precision atomic clocks managed by the International Bureau of Weights and Measures (BIPM). It is highly precise and adjusted periodically with leap seconds to remain aligned with the Earth's rotation.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Greenwich Mean Time (GMT)</strong>: An astronomical timezone calculated based on mean solar time at the Prime Meridian in Greenwich, London. It is a civil timezone used in several countries (such as the UK, Ireland, and West African nations) during the winter months.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      In daily communication and converter tools, GMT and UTC are often used interchangeably because they share the same time hours (UTC+0). However, technical systems rely exclusively on UTC to avoid astronomical fluctuations.
    </p>
  </section>

