  <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
       SECTION 6: GEOPOLITICAL BOUNDARIES
       ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
  <section id="geopolitical-boundaries" class="content-section reveal" aria-label="Geopolitical Boundaries">
    <h2>6. Geopolitical Boundaries &amp; Fractional Offsets</h2>
    <p>
      In practice, time zones rarely match straight longitudinal lines. Instead, governments adjust timezone boundaries to follow state borders, simplify trade with neighboring nations, or maintain political centralization:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>China's Single Time Zone (UTC+8)</strong>: Geographically, the Chinese landmass is wide enough to span five distinct hourly time zones (from UTC+5 in the far west to UTC+9 in the east). In 1949, the government established a single national standard time—Beijing Time (UTC+8)—to promote administrative efficiency and unity. As a result, residents in western cities like Kashgar experience solar noon as late as 3:00 PM, and schools and offices operate on shifted schedules to align with the late morning sunrise.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>India's Half-Hour Compromise (UTC+5:30)</strong>: Spanning over 2,000 miles from east to west, India faced a choice between splitting the nation into two time zones or establishing a single compromise. The government chose Indian Standard Time (IST, UTC+5:30), centered around the 82.5° E longitude meridian in Prayagraj. This half-hour fractional offset maintains a unified national clock while minimizing the difference between solar noon and civil clocks on both coasts.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Newfoundland Standard Time (UTC-3:30)</strong>: The Canadian province of Newfoundland observes a unique half-hour offset. When Canada standardized time zones in the late 19th century, Newfoundland was an independent British dominion. Seeking to align its clocks with its local solar meridian (which lies halfway between the Atlantic and Eastern zones), they established a distinct UTC-3:30 offset, which they maintained even after joining Canada in 1949.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Pyongyang Time (North Korea)</strong>: In 2015, North Korea shifted its clocks back 30 minutes to establish Pyongyang Time (UTC+8:30) to mark the 70th anniversary of liberation from Japan, returning to the historical timezone used before Japanese colonization. However, in 2018, North Korea reverted back to UTC+9:00 to synchronize its clocks with South Korea as a gesture of political realignment.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>The Eucla Anomaly (UTC+8:45)</strong>: A small roadhouse community in Western Australia (Eucla) along the Eyre Highway observes an unofficial, unique fractional timezone called Central Western Standard Time (CWST, UTC+8:45). Established as a compromise for travelers crossing the vast Australian desert, it is observed by a tiny population of roughly 100 people across a 150-mile road corridor.
      </li>
    </ul>

