  <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
       SECTION 7: DATE LINE
       ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
  <section id="date-line" class="content-section reveal" aria-label="International Date Line">
    <h2>7. The International Date Line (IDL)</h2>
    <p>
      The International Date Line (IDL) is situated roughly along the 180° meridian in the Pacific Ocean. It functions as the boundary separating one calendar day from the next, preventing travelers from experiencing cumulative day discrepancies when circumnavigating the globe:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>Traveling Westward (Skip a Day)</strong>: Crossing the IDL going west (e.g., from San Francisco to Tokyo) advances the calendar by one day. A traveler literally "skips" a calendar date. For example, if you cross the line on Tuesday morning, it instantly becomes Wednesday morning on the other side.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Traveling Eastward (Gain a Day)</strong>: Crossing the IDL going east (e.g., from Tokyo to San Francisco) sets the calendar back by one day, allowing travelers to repeat the same calendar date. A flight departure on Friday afternoon from Seoul can arrive in San Francisco on Friday morning, giving the traveler a 30-hour day.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Samoa's Historical 2011 Jump</strong>: Historically, Samoa sat on the eastern (American) side of the Date Line to trade with California. However, as trade shifted toward Australia and New Zealand, Samoa found itself 21 hours behind its primary partners. On December 29, 2011, Samoa jumped across the IDL, skipping Friday, December 30 entirely, and waking up directly on Saturday, December 31. This moved Samoa from UTC-11 to UTC+13. The territory of Tokelau made the same jump to maintain administrative links.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Kiribati's Eastern Loop (UTC+14)</strong>: In 1995, the island nation of Kiribati shifted the position of the IDL. Previously, the line split the nation, placing eastern islands 22 hours behind western islands. By looping the IDL around its eastern islands, Kiribati unified its business week and established the UTC+14 timezone—the first region to welcome a new calendar day.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Nautical Date Line Rules</strong>: In international waters, ships use nautical time zones, which are straight 15-degree bands. The 180th meridian splits the UTC+12 and UTC-12 zones. Ships change their date exactly when passing the 180° line, rather than following civil borders.
      </li>
    </ul>

