  <section id="common-mistakes" class="content-section reveal" aria-label="Common Mistakes">
    <h2>10. Common Time Zone Mistakes</h2>
    <article class="method-card" style="margin-bottom: 1.5rem;">
      <h3>Mistake 1: Hardcoding Offsets</h3>
      <p>
        <strong>Fact:</strong> Hardcoding timezone offsets (e.g., assuming Eastern Time is always UTC-5) results in software failure when clocks shift to daylight saving time (EDT, UTC-4) in March. Applications should always query named timezone entries from the system database.
      </p>
    </article>

    <article class="method-card" style="margin-bottom: 1.5rem;">
      <h3>Mistake 2: Confusing GMT with UTC</h3>
      <p>
        <strong>Fact:</strong> Although GMT and UTC share identical hours, GMT is a civil timezone subject to regional changes, whereas UTC is a scientific reference standard that remains uniform globally.
      </p>
    </article>

    <article class="method-card">
      <h3>Mistake 3: Misreading the International Date Line</h3>
      <p>
        <strong>Fact:</strong> Crossing the Date Line heading west advances the calendar (+1 day). Travelers often fail to account for this shift, booking airport arrivals or hotel stays on incorrect calendar dates.
      </p>
    </article>
  </section>

